{{/* conditional: shell-owned field-set branches gated by a toggle, shown via
     pure CSS (ADR-005 §7). The plugin declared only the intent; the shell owns
     the toggle and the show/hide — no JavaScript.

     The toggle is a form row like any other: what it is on the left at the same
     fixed measure a field's label takes, the control and whatever explains it on
     the right. A gate that looked unlike the fields it gates would read as a
     different kind of thing, and it is not — it is the setting the rest depend
     on.

     The gate and whichever branch is showing are one row, so the row's own
     padding sits outside both. What the branch is decides how close it hangs:
     a note about the state the toggle is in belongs to the toggle and sits under
     it at a helper line's distance, while a field-set the toggle reveals is more
     rows of the form and stands off at a row's. */}}
<div class="verso-conditional py-2 first:pt-0 last:pb-0" data-verso-change-field data-verso-change-name="{{.Name}}" data-verso-change-label="{{.Label}}" data-verso-change-kind="toggle">
  <div class="verso-conditional-gate {{template "verso-field-row-class"}} !py-0">
    {{template "verso-field-label" .LabelView}}
    <div data-verso-control class="{{template "verso-field-control-class"}}">
      <label class="flex w-fit cursor-pointer items-center">{{template "switch.control" .Control}}</label>
    </div>
  </div>
  {{/* A revealed field-set carries its own rows, and a field row zeroes its top
       padding when it is first — so the standing-off is this block's to state. */}}
  <div class="verso-conditional-body mt-5">{{range .Fields}}{{.}}{{end}}</div>
  {{if .Otherwise}}<div class="verso-conditional-otherwise mt-2">{{range .Otherwise}}{{.}}{{end}}</div>{{end}}
</div>
